              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Use the addition rule to find the intersection, then independence to find the two marginal probabilities. Either assignment satisfies the conditions.",
                "marks": []
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      "worked": "By the addition rule,\n$$\\begin{align*}P(A\\cap B)&=P(A)+P(B)-P(A\\cup B)\\\\P(A\\cap B)&=1.1-0.8=0.3.\\end{align*}$$\nLet $x=P(A)$, so $P(B)=1.1-x$. Independence gives\n$$\\begin{align*}x(1.1-x)&=0.3\\\\x^2-1.1x+0.3&=0\\\\(x-0.5)(x-0.6)&=0\\\\x&=0.5\\text{ or }0.6.\\end{align*}$$\nThus $(P(A),P(B))=(0.5,0.6)$ or $(0.6,0.5)$, and $P(A\\cap B)=0.3$. Both assignments have sum $1.1$, product $0.3$ and union probability $1.1-0.3=0.8$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
